**What this covers.** The scaffold content this kit ships to become part of *your* project: the
templates (`process/templates/`, `.claude/templates/`), the role docs and doctrine sheets under
`process/` once you have adapted them, `PROJECT.md`, the bootstrap files you are expected to
replace or delete (`CLAUDE.md`, `AGENTS.md`, `README.md`), and anything else under `kit/` marked
`REPLACE` or `CONFIGURE` in `process/EXTRACTION.md`'s disposition table — in short, anything this
kit expects you to copy, edit, rename, or delete as your project grows into its own thing.

**Why CC0 and not MIT, for this content specifically.** MIT requires its notice be preserved "in
all copies or substantial portions" of the software. That is a reasonable ask for a script someone
imports unmodified; it is the wrong ask for a template file you are meant to edit into
unrecognizability and eventually throw away (`process/EXTRACTION.md`'s own REPLACE/KEEP
disposition axis names exactly this: some files are scaffolding, not permanent fixtures). CC0
removes that friction by design: once a file under this license is part of your project, it carries
no notice obligation forward, nothing to track, nothing to strip out later. Use it, change it,
delete it, and license your own project however you choose — this dedication does not reach
upward into your work.

**What this does NOT cover:** the kit's own mechanism — its scripts, process doctrine, and the
vendored Dev skill set — which stays under `LICENSE` (MIT) for the reason stated there.
